Z||000002||Rex||04-08-2001||09:42 PM||||Hello Dave, im in Beckley. The plug wires did'nt seem to be bad. I also turned the distributor after marking its orginal location.I did'nt put it in the dianosic mode though. I wanted to see how that affected it( made it worse).Still looking<BR>I really enjoy this little gem.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DavidHenshaw||04-08-2001||10:00 PM||||Rex, I looked in the Haynes manual but couldn't find anything similar to your description. My guess would be fuel injection. If you're ever in S Charleston stop by. I'm at 5022 McCorkle Ave just across the river from Dunbar.<P>Dave<P>86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||04-10-2001||03:31 PM||||Had an 86 that did that.. I found that the vacuum line to the MAP sensor had a break in it. If you have another MAP handy just swap them out and try again. On my 88 the symptoms were similar but I found out that the DIS ignition and Bosch Platinum plugs don’t like each other. Apparently when the plugs wear and the gap increases, misfires and backfires are quite troublesome. I had infact mistaken the MAP to be the culprit.... a $60 misdiagnosis.<P><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 04-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||thomas_l||04-10-2001||04:59 PM||||Pull the plug wires off the cap one at a time to see if any of the cap terminals have "cancer" - check the coil wire on both ends too. If everything is clean and shiny, remove the dist cap and check for carbon tracking inside the cap or on the rotor. The rotor may even have a pinhole burned through the center of it. Use a towel to wipe off any crud to get a clear look at it. The tracking under the cap can be caused by condensation under the cap - misfires when cold, but after warming up, the condensation evaporates and the trouble goes away. Either way it sounds like secondary ignition. If all the above checks good you need a good meter and a shop manual or pay someone that has them.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Rex||04-10-2001||07:12 PM||||Thanks for the reccomendations I will look at them all. It could be almost anything causing the engine to miss. I may have to borrow someones Fiero so I'll know how well they are suposed to run. This is my 1st mid engine american sports car. I am interested in installing a V-8 and am gathering info.||||reg||1
